Original commit body from 05c67f3aaec86d566cc2b0fbae24e86ea2546f2c:

INV-dihos phase 6 never scheduled swift: 100 of its 107 catalogued io rows are
method-kind, and a receiver bound to a method-call RESULT (`let s =
store.session()`) was untyped, so those rows had no consumer to feed.

Pass 1 now registers `<Owner>.<func> -> T` for every `func ... -> T`
(`_swift_return_type_name`: an optional is unwrapped, generic arguments are
dropped, and a collection / tuple / closure type is refused -- the catalogue
keys by bare receiver type). The base analyzer aggregates the per-file maps
(first writer wins) into the registry java, kotlin and go already read, and
WI-lalot's loader will feed library rows into the same dict.

Pass 2 reads it at the binding (`_swift_call_result_type`): `store.session()`
through the receiver's type, `Store.make()` through the type head, a bare
`makeSession()` through the enclosing type and then the free function, with
`try` / `await` wrappers unwrapped. An unknown callee leaves the local untyped,
which is what an unknown result means.

The registry fixture -- two functions each declaring `let s` -- exposed that
swift's receiver map was FILE-scoped: a later `let s` overwrote the earlier
one, and any function's parameter typed every same-named receiver in the file
(right by naming convention when `task` is always a URLSessionTask, wrong when
`request` is Alamofire's Request in one function and Foundation's URLRequest in
another). Receiver types are now scoped to the declaring function by the exact
parent chain (kotlin got a scope stack for the same bug; byte-span containment
was tried first and broke on Session.swift, whose error-recovered parse has a
function whose span swallows the class body), file-level declarations stay
visible everywhere, and an optional annotation (`var task: URLSessionTask?`,
`t: URLSessionTask?`) names its wrapped type -- the real declarations the leak
had been standing in for. What the leak covered by luck and no declaration
could -- `session.request(.get)` in a test whose `session` is `private var
session: Session?` in BaseTestCase.swift, another file, one class up -- is now
covered structurally: class-level properties join the base analyzer's
repo-wide field-type registry (`FileAnalysis.class_field_types`, the interface
python / java / rust already use), and a bare receiver no scope or file-level
declaration types is looked up through the enclosing type and its
`base_classes`. Error-recovered files (Session.swift, INV-bisok) get one rule:
a declaration under an ERROR node is file-level, a lookup walks through them
to its real function. (Bisecting Session.swift for that error found INV-bisok's
root cause on the way: a `#if canImport(...)` block inside a class body is not
parsed by tree-sitter-swift; recorded on the item, not fixed here.)

